# ember — environment
#
# Every one of these is optional. With none of them set the app is fully
# usable: prices come from the mock provider and everything you enter stays on
# the device. That is a supported way to run ember, not a broken one — see
# `src/lib/supabase/config.ts` and ADR 0007.

# ── Accounts and sync (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# From the Supabase dashboard: Project Settings → API.
#
# Both are safe in the browser bundle. The anon key is *designed* to be public
# and row-level security is what makes it safe — every table in
# `supabase/migrations/0001_init.sql` has RLS enabled with `auth.uid()`
# policies. Never put the service-role key here or anywhere under `src/`;
# nothing in ember needs it.
#
# Setting these turns on: sign-in, cross-device sync of the ledger,
# preferences and watchlist, and server-stored broadcasts. Leaving them unset
# hides every account control rather than disabling it.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ── Market data ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set `EMBER_DATA_PROVIDER=live` and supply both keys to route prices,
# fundamentals, news and the earnings calendar to real providers. Leave it
# unset and ember runs entirely on generated data — fully usable, and what
# `npm run verify` measures against, since a gate whose numbers move with the
# market is measuring the market.
#
# Both keys are free tiers and neither is public: they are read on the server
# only, never prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, and nothing under `src/components` can
# reach them.
#
#   Finnhub    finnhub.io/register       60 req/min — fundamentals, news,
#                                        search, earnings calendar
#   CoinGecko  coingecko.com/en/api      30 req/min — crypto prices and history
#
# Foreign exchange comes from the ECB via api.frankfurter.dev, which needs no
# key. Equity price history comes from Yahoo's undocumented chart endpoint —
# see the header of `providers/live/yahoo.ts`, and CLAUDE.md §7: **that one has
# to be replaced with a licensed feed before ember charges anybody.**
#
# EMBER_DATA_PROVIDER=live
# FINNHUB_API_KEY=
# COINGECKO_API_KEY==mock


# ── Reaching you with ember closed (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# Everything below is read by `scripts/` and by GitHub Actions, never by the
# app. None of it belongs in `.env.local` unless you are running the senders by
# hand — see `.github/workflows/scheduled.yml` for the deployed arrangement.
#
# **The service-role key is the one credential in this project that can ignore
# row-level security.** It exists so one scheduled script can write `ngx_daily`
# and `digest_log`, which every client is refused. It must never appear under
# `src/`, never be prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, and never be committed — the
# application does not need it and cannot use it.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# Where the deployed app answers. The digest sender asks it for advice over HTTP
# rather than importing the engine, so one assessment cycle serves every
# recipient on a risk profile.
#
# EMBER_APP_URL=https://your-deployment
#
# Lets the sender read every call: /api/advice locks most of them for a request
# without a Pro session. Any long random string, set on the app and the workflow.
#
# EMBER_SENDER_TOKEN=
#
# ── Push ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate a pair once and keep it: rotating them invalidates every existing
# subscription, and every user would have to opt in again.
#
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# The public half is inlined into the browser bundle by design — it is the
# identity a push service checks a signed request against, and it is useless
# without the private half.
#
# Register the service worker in `next dev` too. Off by default because a cached
# shell makes hot reload lie about what is on disk — but push cannot be tried at
# all without a registered worker, so turn it on while you are working on push.
# Push also needs a secure context: localhost counts, a LAN address does not, so
# testing on a phone means `npm run dev:https`.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMBER_SW_IN_DEV=1

# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
#
# ── Email ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resend's free tier is 3,000 emails a month from a verified domain, which is
# ADR 0008's no-spend-before-revenue rule satisfied. `From:` has to be on a
# domain you have verified with them or every message is rejected.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMBER_EMAIL_FROM=ember <alerts@yourdomain>
